Yes, but only by changing the grating or the wavelength, not by changing the angle of observation. The grating equation d sin θ = mλ gives sin θ = mλ/d, and since sin θ cannot exceed 1, the highest order that can be seen is m_max = d/λ (the largest integer not exceeding d/λ).
For a given spectrum, that is for a fixed range of wavelengths, m_max can therefore be increased by:
(i) using a grating of larger spacing d, that is one with fewer lines per centimetre;
(ii) using light of longer wavelength, since m_max is inversely proportional to λ;
(iii) immersing the grating in a medium of refractive index n, because the wavelength inside the medium becomes λ/n, so m_max = nd/λ.
There is a practical limit. As d is increased the angular dispersion dθ/dλ = m/(d cos θ) decreases, adjacent orders move closer together and eventually overlap, so the higher orders become harder to separate and the spectrum becomes less pure. There is also a limit set by the width of the grating and by the intensity of the higher orders. Hence the number of orders can be increased, but only at the cost of dispersion and resolution.

For a single slit of width a the first minimum occurs at sin θ = λ/a, so the angular half-width of the central maximum is θ ≈ λ/a and the width of the central maximum on a screen at distance L is 2λL/a.
As a is slowly decreased:
(i) the central maximum spreads out, because its width is inversely proportional to a; the first minimum moves to a larger angle;
(ii) the secondary maxima also move outward and their spacing increases;
(iii) the intensity of the pattern falls, since less light passes through the narrower slit, and the relative brightness of the secondary maxima compared with the central maximum stays roughly the same (about 4.5% for the first secondary);
(iv) the number of secondary maxima that can be seen on a screen of fixed size decreases.
When a becomes comparable with λ the central maximum fills almost the whole screen and the pattern becomes a broad, slowly varying distribution. When a is much smaller than λ, θ approaches 90° and the slit behaves almost like a point source, sending out a nearly uniform cylindrical wave with no distinct minima. Thus making the slit narrower makes the light spread more, which is the wave-optical statement that a narrower aperture produces a wider beam.
